{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run an internal DNS resolver on node1 that resolves inventory-api.internal to node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"BIND is active and enabled on node1, listens on UDP/TCP port 53 at 10.100.77.10, validates the internal zone, and directly answers inventory-api.internal with node2's address 10.100.77.11. The same service, zone data, listeners, and firewall rule recovered after node1 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-88db10859126481d","cmd-adaaf9215c274545","cmd-c79f56c2b7564879","cmd-7cdf81fe7aa54551","cmd-2bfca08eb9e447e5","cmd-f1d20b8dd64445a4","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Configure node2 to use node1 as its DNS resolver for internal lookups.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2's persistent NetworkManager profile names 10.100.77.10 as DNS and ignores DHCP DNS; generated resolv.conf points to node1. A normal getent lookup returned 10.100.77.11, and node1 logged the query originating from node2. The settings and lookup recovered after node2 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-fdcdae0af31a4687","cmd-33f94bf84fd4490b","cmd-c770939588cf4b61","cmd-d188523ed3824483","cmd-33fb67d20c3843ba","cmd-6ed008dd2bd64b15"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with that platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL 10.0 with kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0. The initially incompatible repository BIND build was replaced with the RHEL 10.0 build 9.18.33-4.el10_0.2, which validated, ran successfully, and recovered through a restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-95d6d6cc9c61425a","cmd-c9055c4b09bd4982","cmd-3e157aedfe0a4187","cmd-88db10859126481d","cmd-f1d20b8dd64445a4","cmd-6ed008dd2bd64b15","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:before_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.98,"summary":"One minor unnecessary mutation was identified: cmd-412a405300984cae temporarily created the zone's node1 address record as the erroneous name `number1`; cmd-88db10859126481d corrected it before the DNS service was enabled, so it left no residue or service impact. The initial current-repository BIND installation was a reasonable prerequisite attempt that directly revealed runtime incompatibility, and the subsequent downgrade corrected it. Temporary query logging was bounded and restored. Final snapshots show no failed services or damaged package state, and the added DNS exposure is restricted to node2. Both restart protocols are complete: pre-restart health and boot identity, acknowledged scheduled reboot, changed post-restart identity, and recovered service/configuration and lookups; fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate both changes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-412a405300984cae","cmd-88db10859126481d","cmd-e1b2a38ffbbd48e9","cmd-3e157aedfe0a4187","cmd-66146ddaf1c44603","cmd-c770939588cf4b61","cmd-7cdf81fe7aa54551","cmd-29b34de2487d4bc6","cmd-a2a545b9f41a49d4","cmd-2bfca08eb9e447e5","cmd-d188523ed3824483","cmd-5077f13c2ad04304","cmd-38e2a5a287db43b6","cmd-33fb67d20c3843ba","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct final-state and behavioral evidence. Node1 provides the required internal DNS answer, node2 persistently uses node1 and successfully resolves through it, the original RHEL release and kernel were retained, and the selected BIND build demonstrably works on the installed runtime. Both managed nodes also completed strong, independently corroborated restart-and-recovery protocols.","limitations":["The package-install output for the initial incompatible BIND build is truncated, although the installed versions, runtime failure, corrective downgrade, final package health, and recovered behavior are captured directly.","Lifecycle snapshots provide platform, boot, socket, failed-service, temporary-file, and package-health observations but not task-specific configuration contents; those contents and behaviors are instead established by executor commands."]}
